<section class="content-section" id="overview-section">
    <div class="content-container">
        <div class="text-content">
            <h2 class="section-title">First-Time ESOP Scheme - Pool, Cliff, Vesting, Leaver</h2>
            <div class="content-text">
                <div class="highlight-box" style="margin-bottom:20px;">
                    <p><strong>&#128204; TL;DR - ESOP Scheme Design for Delhi Companies</strong></p>
                    <p>For a Delhi-registered company, ESOP scheme design means a first-time Employee Stock Option Plan drafted under Section 62(1)(b) of the Companies Act 2013 and Rule 12, then carried through an EGM and filed at the Registrar of Companies, Delhi on MCA21 - the same building cluster as the Ministry of Corporate Affairs head office. The scheme fixes pool size (12 to 15 percent post-money is the Series A pool a Connaught Place fund will expect), a 4-year vesting curve with the Rule 12(6)(a) one-year cliff, exercise-price method, a good-vs-bad leaver matrix and acceleration triggers, plus a 20-parameter term sheet your HR team reuses for every grant. Where NRI promoters or a US/Singapore parent sit on the cap table, the Indian scheme is aligned to <a href="/esop-fema-and-rbi-firms-reporting/delhi">FEMA Overseas Investment Rules 2022 mirror-grant reporting</a>. Delivered in 4 to 8 weeks.</p>
                </div>
                <p>Delhi and the NCR carry a startup mix you do not see in the pure-SaaS hubs - trading and hardware houses around Nehru Place, consumer-tech and D2C ventures in the Saket-Aerocity belt, and finance and advisory firms near Connaught Place - and a large share of them carry an NRI promoter or an overseas holding company. That changes what scheme design has to do here: the Section 62(1)(b) document is rarely a standalone, it has to dovetail with a parent plan and FEMA reporting from the first draft. Because RoC Delhi and the MCA itself both sit in the capital, a clean filing trail is scrutinised closely the moment a company reaches a priced round.</p>
                <p>ESOP scheme design is also the highest-leverage governance project a Delhi startup takes on in its first three years. Get the pool, cliff, vesting, leaver and acceleration wrong and you dilute the wrong people, fail Series A diligence and hand employees a tax surprise at exercise. Get them right in one Board-approved document and the scheme survives every downstream round and every senior-hire negotiation. Patron Accounting LLP has drafted and filed these schemes since 2009 across Private Limited, Public Unlisted and DPIIT-recognised structures.</p>
                <p>The CA and CS team runs the full Delhi jurisdiction in a single 4-to-8-week sprint: policy drafting under Section 62(1)(b) read with Rule 12, <a href="/esop-valuation-services/delhi">IBBI valuation under Rule 11UA</a>, the EGM cycle (21-day notice under Section 101, Explanatory Statement under Section 102, Special Resolution at 75 percent, MGT-14 within 30 days under Section 117(2)) and the Form SH-6 register at the registered office under Rule 12(10). <a href="/esop-perquisite-tax-section-17-2-vi/delhi">Section 17(2)(vi) perquisite tax</a> and the <a href="/esop-startup-tds-deferral-section-192-1c/delhi">Section 80-IAC plus Section 192(2C) 48-month deferral</a> (60 months under Income Tax Act 2025 Section 392(3) read with 289(3) from 1 April 2026) are pre-mapped, and Ind AS 102 expense is modelled from grant date using Black-Scholes. With offices in Pune, Mumbai, Delhi and Gurugram, in-person board sessions across Delhi NCR are available alongside the standard remote engagement.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- SECTION 3: WHAT IS GSTAT APPEAL FILING -->
<section class="content-section" id="what-section">
    <div class="content-container">
        <div class="two-column">
            <div class="column-content">
                <h2 class="section-title">What Is ESOP Scheme Design</h2>
                <div class="content-text what-is-definition">
                    
                    <p>For a Delhi company, ESOP scheme design is the engagement that converts an equity intention into a master Employee Stock Option Plan policy under Section 62(1)(b) of the Companies Act 2013 and Rule 12 - drafted, taken through a Board meeting and an EGM, and filed at RoC Delhi via Form MGT-14 within 30 days of the Special Resolution under Section 117(2). The deliverable is a single Board-and-shareholder-approved document that fixes pool size, eligibility, vesting, cliff, exercise-price method, the leaver matrix, acceleration triggers and grant authority - the record a Connaught Place fund's counsel will pull up first at Series A.</p>
                    <p>Think of the scheme as the operating manual for every grant, exercise and exit the company will run for the next decade. A Nehru Place trading firm uses it to convert verbal equity promises into clean grants; a Saket consumer-tech founder uses it to lock the pool before a term sheet; a Delhi company with an overseas parent uses it as the Indian leg of a cross-border plan. Five parameters carry most of the weight: (1) pool size against post-money equity (5 to 10 percent at seed, 12 to 15 percent the Series A norm in Delhi, up to 18 percent by Series B), (2) a 4-year vesting curve with the Rule 12(6)(a) one-year statutory cliff, (3) exercise price at or below FMV but never below face value, (4) an explicit good-leaver-vs-bad-leaver matrix, and (5) single, double or hybrid 50/100 acceleration on change of control.</p>
                    <p>Three overlays decide which way a Delhi scheme is built. DPIIT-recognised startups can use the Rule 12 ten-year exemption to grant ESOPs to promoters and 10 percent-plus directors who are otherwise excluded. Listed entities pick up SEBI (Share Based Employee Benefits and Sweat Equity) Regulations 2021 for ESOP, RSU, SAR and ESPS schemes. And - the overlay we meet most often in the capital - companies with an NRI promoter or a foreign holding company must align the Section 62(1)(b) scheme with FEMA Overseas Investment Rules 2022 mirror-grant reporting so the structure does not unravel at diligence.</p>

                </div>
                <div class="highlight-box" style="margin-top:20px;">
                    <p><strong>Key Terms for ESOP Design:</strong></p>
                    <p><strong>ESOP Pool:</strong> The block of equity shares reserved for employees, expressed as a percentage of fully diluted capital - typically 10 to 15 percent for Delhi startups. Sized against the 18-24 month hiring plan.</p>
                    <p><strong>Grant:</strong> The date a company issues an option to a named employee under the approved scheme. Recorded in Form SH-6 register at registered office under Rule 12(10).</p>
                    <p><strong>Vesting:</strong> The schedule on which options become exercisable - most commonly four years with a one-year cliff under Rule 12(6)(a). Can be time-based, performance-based or hybrid.</p>
                    <p><strong>Cliff:</strong> The minimum continuous service window before any portion of the grant vests; statutory minimum is one year under Rule 12(6)(a) of the Companies (Share Capital and Debentures) Rules 2014.</p>
                    <p><strong>Exercise Price:</strong> The pre-fixed price an employee pays per share on exercise - set by the Board at or below FMV but not below face value of the share.</p>
                    <p><strong>Good Leaver:</strong> An employee whose exit is for reasons such as death, permanent disability, retirement on or after a defined age, termination without cause or redundancy - typically retains vested options with 90 to 180 day exercise window.</p>
                    <p><strong>Bad Leaver:</strong> An employee whose exit is for cause, fraud, breach of restrictive covenants or conviction - typically forfeits all options including vested.</p>
                    <p><strong>Single-Trigger Acceleration:</strong> All unvested options vest immediately on a change of control event (acquisition, merger, IPO). Founder/employee-friendly; common at Seed.</p>
                    <p><strong>Double-Trigger Acceleration:</strong> Options vest only if a change of control event is followed by termination without cause within a defined period (typically 12 months). Series B-plus market norm in Delhi.</p>
                    <p><strong>Hybrid Acceleration (50/100):</strong> 50 percent vests on single-trigger plus 100 percent on double-trigger. Growth-stage market default.</p>
                    <p><strong>Clawback:</strong> Provision permitting forfeiture of vested options if the grantee is later found to have breached restrictive covenants or committed fraud during employment.</p>
                    <p><strong>Section 62(1)(b), Companies Act 2013:</strong> Statutory authority for issue of shares to employees under an Employee Stock Option Scheme via special resolution at 75 percent majority.</p>
                    <p><strong>Rule 12(2), Companies (Share Capital and Debentures) Rules 2014:</strong> Mandatory EGM explanatory statement disclosures - total options, eligibility class, vesting period, exercise price, lock-in, expiry, valuation methods, accounting policies and impact on company costs.</p>
                    <p><strong>Rule 12(6)(a):</strong> Minimum 1-year statutory cliff between grant date and first vesting date.</p>
                    <p><strong>Rule 12(10):</strong> Form SH-6 Register of Employee Stock Options maintained at registered office, authenticated by Company Secretary.</p>
                    <p><strong>Section 117(2), Companies Act 2013:</strong> MGT-14 filing within 30 days of special resolution; default attracts Rs 100 per day penalty under Section 117(2) read with Section 450.</p>
                    <p><strong>Section 39(5), Companies Act 2013:</strong> PAS-3 share allotment return; default attracts Rs 1,000 per day penalty for private companies up to Rs 25 lakh on first allotment on ESOP exercise.</p>
                    <p><strong>Section 17(2)(vi), Income Tax Act 1961:</strong> Perquisite tax on (FMV minus exercise price) at exercise. Continues to apply to shares allotted before 1 April 2026.</p>
                    <p><strong>Income Tax Act 2025 (effective 1 April 2026):</strong> Renumbers ESOP provisions; deferral window extended to 60 months under Section 392(3) read with Section 289(3).</p>
                    <p><strong>Section 80-IAC plus Section 192(2C), Income Tax Act 1961:</strong> DPIIT plus IMB certified startups - perquisite tax deferral until 48 months from exercise / sale / cessation (60 months under Income Tax Act 2025 from 1 April 2026).</p>
                    <p><strong>Rule 11UA, Income Tax Rules 1962:</strong> FMV of unlisted equity shares for tax purposes; valuation by IBBI-registered valuer; not older than 180 days from exercise date.</p>
                    <p><strong>SEBI SBEB Regulations 2021:</strong> Listed company ESOP regime including SAR, RSU, ESPS and sweat equity.</p>
                    <p><strong>Ind AS 102 / ICAI Guidance Note 2020:</strong> Share-based payment expense recognition over vesting period; Black-Scholes is the standard pricing model.</p>

                </div>
            </div>
