<!-- SECTION 4: WHO CAN FILE -->
<section class="content-section" id="who-section">
    <div class="content-container">
        <div class="text-content">
            <h2 class="section-title">Who Needs ESOP Scheme Design</h2>
            <div class="content-text">
                
                <p>Delhi and the wider NCR run a different kind of startup mix from the pure-SaaS hubs - Nehru Place IT and trading houses, consumer-tech and D2C ventures around Saket and Aerocity, and finance and advisory firms clustered near Connaught Place, many carrying NRI promoters or a foreign holding company on the cap table. ESOP scheme design is the first-time engagement that turns a Delhi unlisted company's equity intentions into a Board-approved, RoC Delhi-filed Employee Stock Option Plan. Because RoC Delhi and the MCA head office both sit in the capital, getting the Section 62(1)(b) sequence right the first time matters even more for companies that will be looked at closely at Series A. The engagement scales from bootstrapped pre-revenue through Series B; listed entities pick up the additional SEBI SBEB Regulations 2021 layer as a premium engagement.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Nehru Place trading and product firms making their first equity hire</strong> - a hardware-trading or B2B distribution company moving from a founder-only cap table to a Seed-stage 5-12 percent pool with 4-year vesting and a 1-year cliff before its first 5-25 grants go out.</li>
                    <li><strong>Saket and Aerocity consumer-tech ventures pre-Series A</strong> - a D2C or consumer-tech founder who needs the scheme on record before the term sheet, since Series A investors typically insist on a 12-15 percent post-money pool established pre-funding; skipping it forces founder dilution at signing.</li>
                    <li><strong>Delhi companies with NRI promoters or a foreign parent</strong> - structures where promoters or the holding company sit abroad need the Indian Section 62(1)(b) scheme aligned with the parent plan and FEMA Overseas Investment Rules 2022 reporting from day one.</li>
                    <li><strong>Connaught Place finance and advisory firms regularising informal equity promises</strong> - two to four years of verbal equity offers to early partners and analysts converted into a clean Board-approved scheme; backdated effective grant dates are allowed where the Board ratifies.</li>
                    <li><strong>Senior CXO hires under negotiation</strong> - VP Engineering, VP Product, CFO or CTO joining at Seed or Series A on equity-weighted offers that need performance vesting hooks and accelerated vesting written into the scheme.</li>
                    <li><strong>DPIIT-recognised Delhi startups granting founder ESOPs</strong> - the Rule 12 10-year founder exemption lets promoters and 10 percent-plus directors receive ESOPs in DPIIT-recognised startups (Private Limited or LLP, within 10 years of incorporation, turnover under Rs 100 crore).</li>
                    <li><strong>Companies whose AoA pre-dates ESOPs</strong> - an older AoA that does not authorise ESOPs needs a fresh EGM Special Resolution and MGT-14 filing at RoC Delhi before the scheme can be adopted.</li>
                    <li><strong>Founders heading into Series A or M&amp;A diligence</strong> - a clean scheme survives Series A through C review and acquirer counsel scrutiny with one minor comment typical.</li>
                    <li><strong>Listed entities under SEBI SBEB Regulations 2021</strong> - listed-company ESOP, RSU, SAR and ESPS schemes carrying grantee class disclosure, Stock Exchange notification and shareholder communication.</li>
                </ul>
                <p><strong>Statutory framework recap:</strong> Section 62(1)(b) of Companies Act 2013 is the statutory authority for issue of shares to employees under an Employee Stock Option Scheme via Special Resolution at 75 percent majority. Rule 12 of Companies (Share Capital and Debentures) Rules 2014 specifies the operational provisions. Rule 12(2) lists mandatory EGM Explanatory Statement disclosures. Rule 12(6)(a) imposes the minimum 1-year statutory cliff. Rule 12(10) requires Form SH-6 register at registered office authenticated by Company Secretary. Section 117(2) requires MGT-14 filing within 30 days of Special Resolution (default Rs 100 per day under Section 450). Section 39(5) requires PAS-3 on first allotment (default Rs 1,000 per day for private companies up to Rs 25 lakh). Section 17(2)(vi) of Income Tax Act 1961 imposes perquisite tax at exercise. Section 80-IAC plus Section 192(2C) provide 48-month deferral for DPIIT plus IMB certified startups (60 months under Income Tax Act 2025 Section 392(3) read with 289(3) from 1 April 2026). Rule 11UA prescribes FMV methodology via IBBI-registered valuer. SEBI SBEB Regulations 2021 govern listed entities. Ind AS 102 plus ICAI Guidance Note 2020 govern share-based payment accounting using Black-Scholes.</p>

            </div>
